{"benchmark": "ifeval", "task_id": "1000", "sample_idx": 3, "model_name": "abacusai/dracarys-llama-3.1-70b-instruct", "content": "Raymond III was the Count of Tripoli from 1152 to 1187. He was the son of Raymond II and Hodierna of Jerusalem. Raymond III was a key figure in the politics of the Crusader states during the 12th century.\n\n**Early Life and Succession**\nRaymond III was born in 1140. His father Raymond II was assassinated in 1152 and Raymond III succeeded him as the Count of Tripoli at the age of 12. His mother Hodierna acted as regent until he came of age.\n\nRaymond III played a significant role in the politics of the Crusader states. He was a close ally of King Amalric I of Jerusalem and participated in several military campaigns against the Fatimid Caliphate. In 1164 Raymond III was captured by the Muslims while on a military campaign and was held prisoner for several years.\n\n**Marriage and Family**\nRaymond III married Eschiva of Bures in 1173. She was the daughter of William of Bures and the widow of Walter of Saint-Omer. The marriage was likely arranged by King Amalric I of Jerusalem who was seeking to strengthen his alliance with the Count of Tripoli.\n\n**Later Life and Death**\nRaymond III died in 1187 at the age of 47. He was succeeded by his godson Raymond IV who was the son of Bohemond III of Antioch and Orgueilleuse of Harcourt. Raymond III's death marked the end of the direct line of the Counts of Tripoli and led to a period of instability in the region.\n\n*The Siege of Harenc*\nIn 1164 Raymond III led a military campaign against the Fatimid Caliphate and was defeated at the Battle of Artah. He was captured and held prisoner for several years. The siege of Harenc was a significant defeat for the Crusaders and marked a turning point in the politics of the region.\n\n*The Regency of Hodierna*\nAfter the death of Raymond II in 1152 his wife Hodierna acted as regent for their son Raymond III. During her regency Hodierna maintained the alliance with King Baldwin III of Jerusalem and continued to support the military campaigns against the Fatimid Caliphate.\n\n*The Marriage of Eschiva of Bures*\nThe marriage of Raymond III to Eschiva of Bures was likely arranged by King Amalric I of Jerusalem. The marriage strengthened the alliance between the Count of Tripoli and the Kingdom of Jerusalem and marked a significant shift in the politics of the region.", "logprobs": [{"token": "Ray", "logprob": -0.004134069196879864}, {"token": "mond", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " III", "logprob": -6.318112355074845e-06}, {"token": " was", "logprob": -1.245502233505249}, {"token": " the", "logprob": -0.07054182887077332}, {"token": " Count", "logprob": -0.015811331570148468}, {"token": " of", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " Trip", "logprob": -3.576279254957626e-07}, {"token": "oli", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " from", "logprob": -0.00033574970439076424}, {"token": " ", "logprob": -0.0010117044439539313}, {"token": "115", "logprob": -1.3232318451628089e-05}, {"token": "2", "logprob": -7.033595466054976e-05}, {"token": " to", "logprob": -0.15505389869213104}, {"token": " ", "logprob": -0.00958858709782362}, {"token": "118", "logprob": -0.00010562501120148227}, {"token": "7", "logprob": -6.914378172950819e-05}, {"token": ".", "logprob": -0.20123690366744995}, {"token": " He", "logprob": -0.0026081211399286985}, {"token": " was", "logprob": -0.0036878555547446012}, {"token": " the", "logprob": -0.11819040030241013}, {"token": " son", "logprob": -0.019432077184319496}, {"token": " of", "logprob": -1.3113108252582606e-05}, {"token": " Raymond", "logprob": -0.0008376768091693521}, {"token": " II", "logprob": -4.410753263073275e-06}, {"token": " and", "logprob": -0.11002334207296371}, {"token": " H", "logprob": -0.012467896565794945}, {"token": "odi", "logprob": -3.814704541582614e-06}, {"token": "erna", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " of", "logprob": -0.0006734015187248588}, {"token": " Jerusalem", "logprob": -0.0013052992289885879}, {